A queue gives a breadth-first version of the same task. Each entry stores a node and the complete path used to reach it. When the node has no children, the path is ready to be saved.

Dry run: starting with `[1]`, visiting the left child `2` creates `[1, 2]`. If `2` has a leaf `4`, the next entry becomes `[1, 2, 4]`, which is added to the answer.

```python
class Solution:
    def allRootToLeaf(self, root):
        if root is None:
            return []

        answer = []
        queue = [(root, [root.data])]
        index = 0

        while index < len(queue):
            node, path = queue[index]
            index += 1

            if node.left is None and node.right is None:
                answer.append(path)
                continue

            if node.left is not None:
                queue.append((node.left, path + [node.left.data]))

            if node.right is not None:
                queue.append((node.right, path + [node.right.data]))

        return answer
```

The queue keeps all waiting paths, so its memory can be large on a wide tree. Time complexity is `O(nh)` in the worst case due to path copying, and extra space is `O(nh)` including the queue.

Here the recursive function receives the path by value. That means the left and right calls get separate lists, so changes in one branch cannot affect the other branch. At a leaf, the current list is already one complete answer.

A single-node tree is also handled correctly: the root has no children, so its one-value path is returned. The original tree is only read.

